  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Monday: A relatively wet system is slowly \npushing through the region, supported by a southwesterly upper \nlevel jet. Rain and high mountain snow will continue over the\nCascades and across the eastern third of WA through this\nevening, then precipitation chances will shift over far Southeast\nWA and North ID overnight into Monday morning. The system \nstalls through much of the day Monday, keeping precipitation \nchances over the central panhandle until another system\napproaches the West Coast Tuesday, kicking the first system\neastward out of the region.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday: Shortwave ridging sets up over the Inland\nNorthwest Tuesday into Wednesday morning in between systems,\nbringing a brief period of dry and stable conditions.\nTemperatures trend cooler through the first half of the week\nwith highs dropping into the low to mid 40s by Tuesday. We've\nbeen seeing warmer than normal temperatures over the last couple\nof weeks, so this cooldown will put us closer to climatological\nnormals for mid November. Lows will trend colder as well, \nfalling into the upper 20s to low 30s by Tuesday night. Fog will\nbe quick to develop under this stable pattern with a moist\nboundary layer in place due to recent rains. Be prepared for\nreduced visibilities Tuesday and Wednesday mornings, and give\nyourself some extra time for your morning commute.\n\nWednesday night through Saturday: The next system dives down the\nWest Coast and tracks inland over Northern CA Wednesday night,\nkeeping the bulk of the moisture and dynamics south of the\nInland Northwest. This pattern of the upper level jet steering \nsystems south of our region is typical of a strong El Nino year.\nDespite the southerly track of the low, some wrap-around\nmoisture will still make it up north, bringing a 30-50% chance\nof precip across the Columbia Basin and east slopes of the\nCascades. Snow levels will range from 2-3 kft Wednesday night\ninto Thursday, bringing potential for light snow to low-ish \nelevation areas. Little to no snow accumulation is expected. \nAs this low exits on Friday, another one will be right on its \nheels, moving in on Saturday. Confidence is low on the track of\nSaturday's system for now. We'll continue to monitor model\nconsensus (or lack thereof) in the coming days. /Fewkes",
